// Block-scaled quantization of one real vector: every group of g consecutive
// values shares one scale s_g, each value is stored as an element code q_i,
// and the reader sees what survives, x̂_i = s_g · q_i (quantization-kernels
// chapter, symmetric case z_g = 0):
//
//   q_i = q(x_i / s_g), saturated at ±q_max,     x̂_i = s_g q_i,
//   b_eff = b + b_s / g   bits per value (the chapter's effective payload).
//
// q rounds to the nearest element value, ties to even. Elements:
// - INT4: the integers −7 … 7 (q_max = 7), as in the chapter's runnable.
// - FP4 E2M1: 0, ±0.5, ±1, ±1.5, ±2, ±3, ±4, ±6 (q_max = 6), the MXFP4 and
//   NVFP4 element (OCP Microscaling Formats v1.0).
// - FP8 E4M3: 1 sign, 4 exponent, 3 mantissa bits, bias 7, subnormals, no
//   Inf, largest finite 448 (Micikevicius et al. 2022).
// Scales, from the block maximum amax = max |x_i|:
// - FP32 or FP16: s_g = amax / q_max, rounded to the scale's format.
// - E8M0 (MX rule, Rouhani et al. 2023, Algorithm 1): s_g =
//   2^(⌊log2 amax⌋ − e_max), e_max the exponent of the element's largest
//   value (2 for E2M1 and INT4, 8 for E4M3); values that land above q_max
//   are clamped.
// - E4M3 with an FP32 tensor scale (NVFP4 rule, NVIDIA 2025, "Pretraining
//   Large Language Models with NVFP4", appendix B): s_T = amax_T / (q_max ·
//   448) over the whole vector, and s_g = e4m3(amax / q_max / s_T) · s_T.
//
// The two vectors are real: layer 12 of Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B (revision 060db64),
// row 0 of the query-projection weight and the 896 values that projection
// receives for the token " mat" in "The cat sat on the mat because it was
// tired." (tools/figure-data/block-scaling-vectors.py regenerates VECTORS).
// The FP32 tensor scale is taken over the shown vector, which stands in for
// the tensor.

